diff:1587635146:1587634098:=604,625d603%0a%3c %0a%3c !!!!Socket inbound%0a%3c %0a%3c '''$socket_in''' - read-only variable to get the description (proto:ip:port format) of the inbound socket (used for receiving the message).%0a%3c \\%0a%3c The variable also offers detailed read-only access to various attributes/sub-fields of the socket, as  '''$socket_in()'''. The sub-fields of the socket are:%0a%3c * ip - the IP part of the socket%0a%3c * port - the port part of the socket%0a%3c * proto - the name of the protocol of the socket (as "UDP", "TPC", etc)%0a%3c * advertised_ip - the advertised IP part of the socket (it may be NULL if no advertising is done on this particlar socket)%0a%3c * advertised_port - the advertised part part of the socket (it may be NULL if no advertising is done on this particlar socket)%0a%3c * tag - the socket internal tag/alias %0a%3c * anycast - if the socket uses an anycast IP or not (returns 0 if not, 1 if yes)%0a%3c For more details on the meaning of these sub-fields, please also read about the [[Documentation.Script-CoreParameters-4-1#toc66|socket definition]].%0a%3c %0a%3c %0a%3c !!!!Socket outbound%0a%3c %0a%3c '''$socket_out''' - read-write variable for reading or changing the outbound socket of the message. Originally (before being written/changed) it will return the same socket description as '''$socket_in''' (the inbound socket will be used as outbound socket also).%0a%3c \\%0a%3c The variable also offers detailed read-only access to various attributes/sub-fields of the socket, as  '''$socket_out()'''. It provides the same sub-fields as the [[#socket_in|$socket_in]] variable.%0a%3c %0a
